1. The invariant, as written
----------------------------
Covered before planting anything: `Card`'s four fields are private, `Card` has
no `Default`, there is no serde/Deserialize/from_str path, no `unsafe` or
`transmute`, and no other module in the crate constructs a `Card`:

  $ grep -rn "Card {" src/ tests/ | grep -v "^src/card_store.rs"
  (no output)
  $ grep -rn "transmute\|unsafe" src/card_store.rs
  (no output)
  $ grep -rn "Deserialize\|serde\|from_str\|from_json" src/card_store.rs
  (no output)
  $ grep -rn "impl Default" src/card_store.rs
  471:impl Default for CardStore {

The one `Default` is on the store, not the card. `VerifiedWitness` has a private
`_private: ()` field and one constructor, `from_verdict`, which matches
`Verified { evidence }` to `Ok` and every other variant to
`Err(VerdictNotVerified)`. Because the witness type and `Card` live in the same
module and the fields are not `pub`, no other module in the crate can assemble
either one directly. That is a real type-level barrier, not a convention.
2. One test read closely: test_unverified_verdict_cannot_construct_card
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
What is on the left: two verdicts built inside the test -- `Verdict::Failed
{counterexample: {x: 2.0}, expected: 3.0, got: 99.0}` and
`Verdict::Unverifiable { reason: "domain contains pole at x = 0" }` -- and two
calls, `VerifiedWitness::from_verdict(&v)` and `Card::from_verified(p, a, &v)`.
What is on the right: `is_err()`, plus `VerdictNotVerified { reason }` whose
string must contain "Failed" or "Unverifiable".

So the oracle here is the type signature and the spec, not an external library:
the test asserts the *specification* "a non-Verified verdict cannot produce a
witness". That is legitimate for a type-system invariant, but it is a property
test, not a value comparison, and the numbers in the Failed verdict (2.0, 3.0,
99.0) are illustrative -- nothing in the test would notice if they were 2.0,
3.0, 3.0. The message check (`reason.contains("Failed")`) does tie the error to
the variant that caused it, which is what makes plant P1 (below) fail rather
than silently pass.

The test's last block is weaker than it looks: it creates an empty store and
asserts `len() == 0`. It does not attempt an unverified insert, because the type
system will not let one be written -- which is the point, but it means the
"store cannot hold an unverified card" half is proven by absence of a
constructor, not by a call that is refused at run time.

3. Plants (mine)
----------------
Three plants in build/b8r, each applied to a pristine copy of
mathd/src/card_store.rs (sha256 cbe7a7bb...), rebuilt, run, then restored.
Their own commit message says freebuff planted p1 and p2; these are neither of
those: I picked my own targets from the spec sentence "no path builds a Card from
a non-Verified verdict" and "a lookup returns the card asked for".

  $ python plants8.py p1_card_from_any_verdict
  planted p1_card_from_any_verdict
  $ cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip1 --test card_store
  $ node corpus/wasi-runner.mjs .../card_store-*.wasm --nocapture
  test test_unverified_verdict_cannot_construct_card ...
  thread 'main' (1) panicked at tests\card_store.rs:55:5:
  Card::from_verified must reject Verdict::Failed
  RUN_EXIT=1

P1 makes `Card::from_verified` build the card itself with zero evidence instead
of going through `VerifiedWitness::from_verdict`. A Failed or Unverifiable
verdict would then produce a "verified" card. Caught, by the assertion whose
message names the constructor. This is the "path that builds a Card from a
non-Verified verdict" the queue asked for.

  $ python plants8.py p2_lookup_wrong_card
  planted p2_lookup_wrong_card
  $ node corpus/wasi-runner.mjs .../card_store-*.wasm --nocapture
  test test_100k_card_cache_hit_lookup_under_1ms ... --- B8 100k Card Benchmark ---
  thread 'main' (1) panicked at tests\card_store.rs:375:9:
  assertion `left == right` failed
    left: Integer(51796)
   right: Integer(0)
  RUN_EXIT=1

P2 makes `CardStore::get` return the last card of an arbitrary bucket instead of
the bucket for the requested hash. Caught by the benchmark's
`assert_eq!(card.answer(), &Expr::Integer(idx as i64))`: the lookup for card 0
returned a card whose answer is 51796. This is the "lookup that returns the
wrong card" plant.

  $ python plants8.py p3_lookup_uncanonical_key
  planted p3_lookup_uncanonical_key
  $ node corpus/wasi-runner.mjs .../card_store-*.wasm test_structural_hash_cache_keying_canonical_equivalence --nocapture
  test test_structural_hash_cache_keying_canonical_equivalence ...
  thread 'main' (1) panicked at tests\card_store.rs:190:38:
  hit by canonical equivalent
  RUN_EXIT=1

P3 keys `get_by_expr` on the raw AST shape hash instead of the B2 canonical
hash, so "2 + x" stops finding the card stored for "x + 2". Caught by the
canonical-equivalence test. This one guards the "keyed by the B2 structural
hash" half of the spec.

All three restored: `cp src/card_store.rs.pristine src/card_store.rs`, rebuild,
6 passed, and sha256 back to cbe7a7bb....

4. The benchmark, re-run here
-----------------------------
The commit reports 1.36 s to build 100k cards, 152 us cold, 3.6-7.5 us warm and
4.47 us per batch lookup. My run in the wasm32 build of the same test:

  Built 100000 card store in 289.8496ms
  Single lookup for card #0    : 94.7µs (94700 ns)
  Single lookup for card #42   : 3.8µs (3800 ns)
  Single lookup for card #10000: 2.5µs (2500 ns)
  Single lookup for card #50000: 2µs (2000 ns)
  Single lookup for card #99999: 1.9µs (1900 ns)
  Batch duration (10000 queries): 9.6682ms; Average per lookup: 966ns (966 ns)
  --- Benchmark complete: PASS (< 1 ms requirement satisfied) ---

Same shape, faster machine/profile; the commit's numbers are plausible and mine
are not a contradiction, but they are not reproducible from the log as written
(there is no record of the machine, profile or target in bb3adf9's message). The
assertion that matters -- `query_duration < Duration::from_millis(1)` for five
probe points and the same bound on the 10,000-query average -- is real and runs
on every `cargo test`. The build time and the published microsecond figures are
not asserted anywhere.

5. What the tests do not cover
------------------------------
N1 (invariant does not cover FailedDomain). V8 added `Verdict::FailedDomain`
after B8, and `card_store.rs` handles it through the `_` catch-all:

  card_store.rs:98  // Catch any future non-verified verdict variant (e.g. FailedDomain in V8)
  card_store.rs:99  #[allow(unreachable_patterns)]

That arm is correct today. No test constructs a `FailedDomain` verdict, and I
planted one that accepts it, in an archive of main (build/b8m), leaving the
Failed and Unverifiable arms alone:

  $ (plant: add `Verdict::FailedDomain { .. } => Ok(Self { evidence: points 0, ... })`
     ahead of the catch-all in VerifiedWitness::from_verdict)
  $ cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip1 --test card_store
  $ node corpus/wasi-runner.mjs .../card_store-*.wasm
  test test_100k_card_cache_hit_lookup_under_1ms ... ok
  test test_differing_shapes_do_not_promote ... ok
  test test_method_card_candidate_promotion_and_never_autopromotes ... ok
  test test_structural_hash_cache_keying_canonical_equivalence ... ok
  test test_unverified_verdict_cannot_construct_card ... ok
  test test_verified_verdict_constructs_card_with_exact_evidence ... ok
  test result: ok. 6 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.53s

A verdict that never verified anything builds a "verified" card and every B8
test stays green. The B8 "done when" criterion is "a test demonstrates no code
path in the crate can insert an unverified card"; with V8 on main that
demonstration no longer covers every verdict variant. This does not fault
bb3adf9 (FailedDomain did not exist when it was written); it is a gap that V8
opened in B8's guarantee, and it is the most serious thing in this review.
Whoever holds mathd now (A1 after F1.5) should add: a `FailedDomain` verdict in
`test_unverified_verdict_cannot_construct_card`, and a unit test that maps every
`Verdict` variant through `VerifiedWitness::from_verdict` and asserts exactly
one `Ok`. I did not write it: this is a review.

N2 (last-wins in `get` is untested). `get` is documented as "Returns the most
recently inserted card for that structural hash", but every test except the
benchmark keeps one card per hash, and the benchmark uses distinct problems
(x + i), so no test ever puts two cards under one key. A `get` that returned the
oldest card would pass the suite. Plant P2 failed only because the answers
differed across buckets, not because last-wins was checked.

N3 (promotion threshold 0 is clamped, untested). `with_promotion_threshold(0)`
becomes 1, so the first insert emits a proposal. No test covers the clamp, and
the doc comment does not mention it.

N4 (method ids and card keys are both u64 hashes). `method_cards` is keyed by
`StructuralShape::shape_hash()`, a DefaultHasher (SipHash) value, and `cards`
by the B2 `structural_hash`. Two different shapes that collided would silently
share a method card. Same class of risk the B2 key already accepts, negligible
in practice, noted for completeness only.

N5 (promotion counts shapes, not answers). `insert` increments the exemplar list
for a shape no matter what answer the card carries, so three cards of shape
($C * $x) + $C with three different answers still produce one "candidate
method". That is probably intended (the method is the shape), but the test uses
one answer (`0`) for all five cards, so the suite does not pin the intended
behaviour either way.

6. What I could not do
----------------------
- Run the two compile-fail doctests myself (no native linker on this host). They
  ran in CI on the same bytes and passed; I cite that run rather than claiming a
  local result.
- Reproduce the commit's exact timings: it does not record machine, profile or
  target, so only the 1 ms assertion is comparable, and that one passes.
- Exercise the store from another crate. The invariant is about what is reachable
  from outside the module; I proved it by reading visibility and grepping for
  constructors, not by writing an external crate that tries to build a Card.
- Judge B8's interaction with the kernel or the ledger. Out of scope here.

7. Findings summary
-------------------
Closed by this review: none of the earlier findings applied (B8 had not been
reviewed before).
Open, for others:
- N1: with V8 on main, a `FailedDomain` verdict can be mapped to a verified card
  and all six B8 tests still pass (plant transcript in section 5). Needs a test,
  and arguably a way to make the compiler demand exhaustiveness instead of the
  `_` arm plus `#[allow(unreachable_patterns)]`.
- N2: `get`'s last-wins behaviour is untested.
- N3: threshold 0 clamping untested.
- N4, N5: notes, no action requested.

VERDICT: PASS for bb3adf9. The type barrier is real (private fields, no Default
on Card, no deserialisation path, no other constructor in the crate), all six
tests pass, the three plants I wrote are each caught with a failure that names
the broken case, and the 1 ms lookup assertion is genuine. Two findings must be
raised against the current tree rather than the commit: N1, where a
`FailedDomain` verdict built by V8 can enter the card store with every B8 test
green, and N2, last-wins lookup never exercised. N1 is the one I would not ship
past: the project invariant "nothing unverified becomes permanent" is exactly
what it breaks.
